Banking System Concurrent

This project simulates a banking system with concurrent transactions, supporting operations such as deposits, withdrawals, money transfers, and account management across multiple branches.

Features

Multi-threaded transaction execution

Thread-safe operations using ReentrantLock

Efficient handling of concurrent requests

Simulated bank branches with account management
Added various banding operation which we can simulate on --->
0 --->for account balance check 
1 ---> add money in bank account
2---->withdraw cash from account 
3----> transfer money from one account of one branch to another account of same/different branch 
4----> add customer to a branch of the bank 
5----> delete account from any branch 
6----> transfer account 


How to Run

To compile and run the banking system, ensure you have BankSystem.java,

Compilation
javac BankSystem.java


Execution

java BankSystem <No_of_Requests>



Example
javac BankSystem.java
java BankSystem 10000


Output
The output will display various transactions such as deposits, withdrawals, and transfers, along with execution time.


Explanation


Initialization:

The system creates 10 bank branches.

Each branch initializes 10,000 customer accounts with random balances.

Transactions are executed concurrently using multiple threads.










Transaction Processing:

Each transaction (deposit, withdrawal, transfer, etc.) is represented as a task executed by a thread pool.
Choice of task is done by a random no generator with given proability distribution 
    if (r <= 0.3) return 0;
        if (r <= 0.53) return 1;
        if (r <= 0.76) return 2;
        if (r <= 0.99) return 3;
        if (r <= 0.993) return 4;
        if (r <= 0.996) return 5;
        return 6;
ReentrantLock is used to prevent race conditions while modifying account balances.

Money transfers involve locking both source and destination accounts to ensure atomicity.











Concurrency Control:

Multiple transactions are processed in parallel using ExecutorService.

Each branch has its own thread pool to handle transactions locally.

Locks are implemented to ensure data consistency while allowing high concurrency.









Performance Considerations:

The program is optimized to minimize locking overhead.

Transactions are evenly distributed across multiple branches to prevent bottlenecks.

The number of threads is chosen based on system capabilities to maximize throughput.